/-!
# The Boolean model

SYNOPSIS item 113 gives the basic formulas for the Boolean model.

The closed subsets of a locally compact, Hausdorff, second countable space carry the σ-algebra
`MSSS.fellSigma` generated by the sets `{F : F ∩ C = ∅}` for compact `C`; this is the Borel
σ-algebra of the Fell topology. It is used as a local instance. A random closed set is a
measurable map into `Closeds E`, and its law is a probability measure on `Closeds E`.

A Boolean model (`MSSS.IsBooleanModel`) is given by germs `x_n`, `n < κ`, forming a stationary
Poisson process of intensity `λ` in `ℝ^d`, and grains `Ξ_n` that are independent with law `Q`
and independent of the germs; `Q` is concentrated on compact sets and
`E V_d(Ξ_0 ⊕ K) < ∞` for compact `K`. The union set is `MSSS.booleanSet`.

The contact distribution function for a structuring element `B` containing the origin is
`H_B(r) = P(0 ∈ Ξ ⊕ rB | 0 ∉ Ξ) = 1 - P(0 ∉ Ξ ⊕ rB) / (1 - p)`. The identifiability statement
of item 113 is for almost surely nonempty grains.

The Boolean model proofs use Poisson marking and count laws, and Mathlib’s compact-set
pointwise sums, product measures, and translation invariance of volume. Identifiability uses
uniqueness of measures on a generating pi-system and the extreme value theorem.

## Main definitions

* `MSSS.IsBooleanModel P κ x G λ Q`, `MSSS.booleanSet κ x G ω`, `MSSS.grainMeanVolume Q K`,
  `MSSS.contactDistribution`.

## Main results

* `MSSS.boolean_capacity`, `MSSS.boolean_hitCount`, `MSSS.boolean_volumeFraction`,
  `MSSS.boolean_covariance`, `MSSS.boolean_contactDistribution`, `MSSS.boolean_identifiable`:
  SYNOPSIS item 113.
-/

public section

noncomputable section

